# Team 4 - Task split (draft)

A possible way to divide the work. Everyone can work on their own part while we agree on the details. Existing files can be handed over for further changes without removing previous work.

Use the shared AWS resources available to the team. The final application hosting option still needs to be agreed.

## Rolands - Website, Docker, NGINX and Discord

**Technologies:** HTML, CSS, JavaScript, Docker, Docker Hub, NGINX, Discord Webhooks.

### DO

- Maintain the Slice & Sip website, menu and order functionality.
- Add Discord notifications for orders, with the webhook URL stored as a secret.
- Maintain application Dockerfile(s), container ports and local Docker tests.
- Define application routes and health-check endpoints.
- Configure NGINX reverse proxy if the agreed hosting setup uses it.
- Provide the working application image and its run instructions to the CI/CD owner.

### DON'T

- Do not take over Terraform, AWS permissions or monitoring. These belong to Aivis after the team agrees on the handover.
- Do not edit Jenkins pipeline stages. Ronalds handles CI/CD.
- Do not build the common deployment script. Svetlana handles orchestration.

### AWS setup ideas

- For a static website: S3 + CloudFront for HTML, CSS and JavaScript.
- For orders requiring server-side logic: API Gateway + Lambda, with Discord Webhooks.
- For container hosting: use an approved managed container service if it is available in the shared AWS environment.

## Aivis - Terraform, AWS infrastructure and CloudWatch

**Technologies:** Terraform, AWS IAM, SSM, VPC, CloudWatch, S3, CloudFront, optional API Gateway/Lambda.

### DO

- Take over further development of the existing Terraform configuration after agreeing with Rolands.
- Check the available AWS permissions, IAM instance profile, networking and SSM access.
- Add and verify stable Terraform outputs needed by other parts, including `instance_id` where relevant and `public_ip`.
- Review the existing infrastructure setup script (`user_data.sh`) together with Terraform; update it only when the chosen setup needs it.
- Prepare infrastructure resources for the hosting option agreed with the team.
- Configure CloudWatch log groups, useful metrics and alarms.
- If supported by the shared account, configure S3 remote Terraform state and a suitable locking method.
- Prepare a safe, automated cleanup step for resources created by the project, with confirmation before shared-resource deletion.
- Run `terraform fmt`, `validate` and `plan`, then test deployment and outputs in the permitted environment.

### DON'T

- Do not delete or replace Rolands' previous commits. Continue from the existing infrastructure and coordinate the handover.
- Do not change application code, Dockerfiles, NGINX routes or Discord logic. Rolands owns those.
- Do not edit Jenkins pipeline logic. Ronalds owns it.
- Do not rewrite Svetlana's common deployment script. Provide stable outputs and commands for it to call.

### AWS setup ideas

- Static website: provision S3 + CloudFront and deployment permissions through Terraform.
- Orders/API: provision API Gateway, Lambda execution permissions and CloudWatch logs if this option is chosen.
- For permitted operations: configure IAM/SSM access to the shared EC2 environment, without assuming it hosts the application.

## Ronalds - Jenkins and CI/CD

**Technologies:** Jenkins, GitHub Webhooks, Docker Hub, AWS CLI, Jenkins Credentials.

### DO

- Maintain Jenkinsfile, Jenkins jobs and build/deployment stages.
- Build and publish versioned Docker images to Docker Hub.
- Set up credentials and GitHub webhook triggers.
- Agree on the main image publishing route to avoid duplicate release builds in GitHub Actions and Jenkins.
- Deploy to the agreed hosting target and check that the deployment actually succeeded.
- Add blue/green or version switching with health checks if the hosting solution supports it.
- Keep build and deployment logs accessible, and pass runtime logging needs to the infrastructure owner.

### DON'T

- Do not change Terraform or AWS role/network configuration. Aivis owns it.
- Do not change the app, Dockerfile or NGINX routes. Rolands owns them.
- Do not create a second overall deployment script. Svetlana handles it.

### AWS setup ideas

- S3 + CloudFront: publish frontend files to S3 and invalidate the CloudFront cache if needed.
- API Gateway + Lambda: coordinate packaging and release versions with the application and infrastructure owners.
- Managed containers: deploy a tagged image to the approved target. Add blue/green only after basic deployment works.

## Svetlana - Common deployment script and Ansible

**Technologies:** Bash, Terraform CLI, Ansible, AWS CLI, SSM, Jenkins CLI/API.

### DO

- Create one common deployment script that starts the existing project components in the agreed order.
- Current setup: Terraform -> EC2 -> `user_data.sh` -> Docker -> Web application. Ansible and Jenkins have separate execution paths.
- Proposed workflow: `deploy.sh` -> Terraform -> EC2 -> Ansible -> Jenkins -> Deployment check.
- Check AWS account/region, required commands, parameters and outputs before running a step.
- Call Terraform, Ansible and Jenkins where applicable, using their existing interfaces.
- Verify real completion status, stop on errors and show readable execution logs.
- Maintain Ansible for the permitted host/server configuration tasks.
- Test the handoff between components, record integration issues and send required fixes to each component owner.
- Provide short instructions for running the common script.

### DON'T

- **DO NOT MODIFY OTHER TEAM MEMBERS' CONFIGURATION FILES.**
- Do not implement Terraform resources in Bash. Aivis owns infrastructure.
- Do not duplicate Jenkins build or deployment logic. Ronalds owns CI/CD.
- Do not change the website, Dockerfile, NGINX routing or Discord integration. Rolands owns the application.

### AWS setup ideas

- S3 + CloudFront: trigger the agreed Terraform/Jenkins commands, then check the published URL.
- API Gateway + Lambda: check the deployed endpoint with a safe request.
- Managed container target: pass the required deployment parameters and wait for the owner-defined healthy status.
- If no host needs setup, skip Ansible rather than using it to configure an unrelated service.

## Rules for parallel work

### DO

- Work on your own component now. Local tests and test inputs are fine while other parts are unfinished.
- Agree on AWS region, image tags, ports, resource outputs, API routes and health-check URLs.
- If another component needs a change, ask its owner to update it.
- Hand over existing files for further maintenance by agreement. Keep the existing work and commit history.

### DON'T

- Do not wait for everyone before starting.
- Do not modify someone else's configuration without agreement.
- Do not run simultaneous Terraform changes or competing deployments against the same shared resources.
- Do not commit credentials, local AWS settings or Terraform state.

## Hosting and additional work

- Static option: S3 + CloudFront, with API Gateway + Lambda only if order handling needs backend logic.
- Container option: use an available approved managed service if running containers is required.
- Extra work: NGINX and application health checks - Rolands; CloudWatch, remote state and cleanup - Aivis; Jenkins webhooks, credentials, image versions and blue/green - Ronalds; common script and end-to-end checks - Svetlana.

**Main idea:** keep working independently, agree on the connections between components, and only then run everything together. The common script executes the steps; it does not rewrite anyone else's configurations.
